            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
p { margin: 4px 0 10px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 486)</h2>
<p><b>Each shot's stand-in is encoded through that shot's own color space.</b></p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed: optimizing a session encoded every shot through ONE color space</b> — whichever the shot on screen happened to be using. Not all EXRs start in the same space, so this was wrong in principle; in practice one stray value baked a transform from the wrong space into all twelve stand-ins at once and clipped their highlights. Each shot now encodes through its own pinned space, falling back to the default for its format.</li>
<li><b>A stand-in encoded from a different space than its shot now declares is treated as stale</b>, exactly like one whose source has been re-rendered: it stops being used, the playlist stops marking it ready, and re-optimizing replaces it. Stand-ins made before this build that were encoded through the wrong space will drop out on their own — <b>re-optimize to replace them.</b></li>
<li><b>New: quitting with unsaved changes asks first.</b> A session lives in memory until you save it, so quitting used to discard per-clip color spaces, grades, marks, notes and trims without a word. Cancelling the save panel now cancels the quit rather than losing the work.</li>
<li>Settings ▸ Color ▸ Looks directory has a Choose… button, and its field no longer wraps its placeholder into three lines.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
